Kubernetes StatefulSet & DaemonSet

Today I learned about StatefulSet and DaemonSet in Kubernetes and when they are used for different types of workloads.

StatefulSet
Used for stateful applications that need stable identity, storage, or ordering.
Commonly used for databases, Kafka, and other stateful workloads.
Pods get stable names such as:
mysql-0, mysql-1, mysql-2
Pods can have persistent storage associated with them.
If a Pod is recreated, Kubernetes can maintain its identity.
StatefulSet
    ↓
mysql-0 → Storage
mysql-1 → Storage
mysql-2 → Storage
DaemonSet
Ensures that one Pod runs on every eligible Node in the cluster.
If a new Node joins, the DaemonSet automatically creates a Pod on it.
Commonly used for logging agents, monitoring agents, security agents, and networking components.
Node 1 → Agent Pod
Node 2 → Agent Pod
Node 3 → Agent Pod
Node 4 → Agent Pod
Key Difference
Deployment  → Fixed number of replicas
StatefulSet → Stable identity + persistent state
DaemonSet   → One Pod per eligible Node